          "content": "That resolves my earlier concern — `stepSchema` defaults `completed` to `true`, so `{ step }` from the client correctly marks the step complete and advances `currentStep`. The design is sound.\n\nI now have a complete picture. Let me write up the analysis.\n\n## Helpkey — Codebase & Onboarding Page Analysis\n\n### Project overview\n\nHelpkey is a hotel booking + partner management platform built on **Next.js 16.3.3 (App Router) with React 19**, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS v4. It's a freelance project with a mature, production-oriented architecture.\n\n**Stack & infrastructure**\n- **Frontend/SSR**: Next.js App Router, React Query (`@tanstack/react-query`) for data hydration, `lucide-react` icons.\n- **Backend**: Firebase — Firestore (data), Firebase Admin (server auth), plus Firebase client SDK. Auth is phone/OTP based via WhatsApp (`WHATSAPP_OTP_API_*`).\n- **Media/storage**: Cloudflare R2 + AWS S3 SDK, `sharp` for image processing.\n- **Async work**: BullMQ + Redis (`ioredis`) with dedicated worker processes for media processing and Firestore→search \"projections\" (dispatcher/runner pattern under `workers/`).\n- **Observability**: OpenTelemetry (`instrumentation.ts`).\n- **Compliance**: A Cashfree-based Aadhaar verification lab (`lib/aadhaar-lab.ts`, `/admin/aadhaar-lab`) with encryption + HMAC rate-limiting, gated behind server-only env vars.\n- **Testing**: Vitest (unit) + Playwright (e2e).\n- **Deploy**: Docker + Caddy, standalone Next output.\n\n**App structure**\n- `app/` — routes: public (`hotels`, `search`, `booking`, `trips`, `wishlist`, `profile`, `help`), `partner/` (onboarding + dashboard workspace), `admin/`, and `api/`.\n- `components/` — feature-grouped UI (partner, hotel, booking, search, admin, etc.).\n- `lib/` — domain services (bookings, reviews, projections, r2, currency, partner service, auth, api handler wrapper).\n\n### The page in the screenshot — Partner Onboarding, Step 4 (Rooms & rates)\n\n**Route chain**: `/partner/onboarding?propertyId=...` → `app/partner/(workspace)/onboarding/page.tsx` renders `<PartnerOnboarding>` (`components/partner/partner-onboarding.tsx`), which for a specific `propertyId` renders `<PropertySetup>` (`components/partner/property-setup.tsx`, ~1330 lines — this is the file that produces the exact UI you see).\n\n**The 8-step wizard** (`PropertySetup`): Property type → Location → Details → **Rooms & rates** → Facilities → Photos → Verification → Review. It maps to the stepper, the \"Step 4 of 8\" badge, the \"Your listing / Draft mode / 38% steps complete\" sidebar, and the \"Need help?\" card — all in one component's layout.\n\n**State & persistence model** (`PropertySetup`, lines 38–115):\n- Loads the full `Listing` from `GET /api/partner/properties/[propertyId]` (property + roomTypes + ratePlans + policies + media + documents in one parallel Firestore fetch).\n- `saveStep(patch)` does an ordered two-step write: `PATCH` the property, *then* `POST .../steps` to mark the step complete. The sequencing is deliberate (commented) so a validation/write failure doesn't advance a draft with rejected data.\n- Guardrail: a draft can revisit completed steps but cannot jump ahead of `onboarding.currentStep` (`goToStep`, and clamping in `load`).\n- The \"38% / 3 of 8\" shown means steps 1–3 are complete and the user is now on step 4 (not yet counted).\n\n**The Rooms & rates step itself** (`RoomsRates`, lines 891–991) — the card titled \"Add your first room and rate\":\nIt enforces a 3-substep sequence to produce one *sellable* room:\n1. **Create a room type** → `POST .../room-types`. On success it auto-scrolls/focuses the policy form. (This is the section with the green check and the \"Room name / Short description\" fields visible in your screenshot.)\n2. **Set a cancellation promise** → `POST .../cancellation-policies` (disabled until a room exists).\n3. **Add a nightly rate** → `POST .../rate-plans`, linking room + policy, converting rupees to paise (`* 100`), and generating an internal rate code (`internalRateCode`).\n\nCompletion logic is well thought out: a room only counts as sellable when a rate plan links it to a cancellation policy. `roomsMissingRates` drives the amber warning and the dynamic \"Continue\" button label, and `roomsAndRatesComplete` gates advancing to Facilities.\n\n**Backing API** (`app/api/partner/properties/[propertyId]/...`): Each route uses a `withApiHandler` wrapper (auth mode, rate limiting, caching), authenticates via Firebase, authorizes via `propertyOwner()` (checks `propertyMemberships/{propertyId}_{uid}` for an active owner, with a 60s Redis cache bypassed in \"strict\" mode), validates with Zod schemas in `lib/partner/service.ts`, writes to Firestore in a batch, enqueues a `property_search` projection, and invalidates public caches.\n\n### Observations\n\nStrengths:\n- Clean separation: thin route handlers → shared `withApiHandler` → Zod validation → Firestore batch + projection enqueue + cache invalidation. Consistent across all room/rate/policy routes.\n- Thoughtful UX for a hard flow: ordered writes, sequential sub-steps, auto-focus, precise completion gating, and customer-friendly error mapping (`customerError` in rate-plans, `validationMessage` in the property route).\n- Good accessibility touches: `aria-busy`, `aria-live`, `aria-current=\"step\"`, `role=\"alert\"`/`role=\"status\"`.\n\nMinor points worth noting (not bugs):\n- `property-setup.tsx` is very large (~1330 lines) and packs many step components plus a big JSX layout into one file. It's readable but would benefit from splitting per-step components into separate files for maintainability.\n- The `steps` route relies on `stepSchema`'s `completed` defaulting to `true`; the client sends only `{ step }`. This is correct but implicit — the coupling is easy to miss.\n- Room creation hardcodes `bedConfigurations: [{ bedType: \"double\", count: 1 }]`, `maxChildren: 0`, `maxInfants: 0` in the onboarding path, so the simplified onboarding form can't express other bed setups (likely intentional for a fast first-room flow; richer editing lives in the dashboard's `partner-rooms-rates-view.tsx`).\n\nI focused this analysis on the onboarding flow shown in the screenshot and its direct dependencies, which I read in full. I did not exhaustively read the booking, search, admin, or worker subsystems — I can dig into any of those next if you'd like.",
